--- a/research/p01-p03-foundations/design.md	1970-01-01 00:00:00.000000000 +0000
+++ b/research/p01-p03-foundations/design.md	2026-08-05 19:27:55.205358300 +0000
@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+# Design: semantic identity, provenance, and lawful merge
+
+## One kernel, three responsibilities
+
+P01-P03 are implemented as one dependency chain. Identity defines when two records may collapse. Provenance defines which records must be retained. Merge then becomes a total, deterministic operation over those records.
+
+```text
+Typed value -> domain/version fingerprint -> Fact / Derivation / Observation
+                                               |
+                                               v
+                                 State = sets of full record variants
+                                               |
+                                               v
+                                   Join = componentwise union
+                                               |
+                                               v
+                             Verify -> ProofBundle -> selected View
+```
+
+## P01 choices
+
+The encoder does not hash arbitrary Go structs. Callers construct a small typed value algebra: null, bool, UTF-8 string, bytes, signed and unsigned integers, finite float, ordered list, unordered set, object, optional value, and portable relative path. Kind tags and length prefixes prevent ambiguous concatenation. Objects sort field names; sets sort and deduplicate encoded elements; lists preserve order. SHA-256 is applied after a fixed package prefix, domain, version, and canonical value.
+
+A field catalog classifies every input as semantic, lineage, observation, presentation, operational, or secret. The mutation harness checks the executable consequence of that classification: fields declared relevant must change identity and fields declared irrelevant must not.
+
+## P02 choices
+
+A fact ID is determined only by `(kind, schema, canonical payload)`. Derivations and observations have independent IDs. Several derivations may support one fact without duplicating it. The verifier checks content-address integrity, dependency closure, observation subjects, identity conflicts, and finite well-founded proofs. Its rank computation is a least fixed-point iteration: seeds receive rank zero; a derived fact receives one plus the maximum rank of the inputs of its cheapest available proof.
+
+Proof bundles contain only selected facts, their transitive dependencies, every supporting derivation among those facts, and related observations. A separate verifier can check the bundle without invoking a provider.
+
+## P03 choices
+
+A state maps each nominal ID to a set of complete record variants. Join is componentwise set union. This makes it associative, commutative, idempotent, and gives the empty state as identity. If two different payloads claim the same ID, both survive. Normal consumers cannot resolve a conflicted ID as unique, and verification reports an identity conflict.
+
+The pure `Join` clones state and is useful as a specification. `Ledger` mutates behind a mutex but performs the same union. The mutex supplies memory safety and a linearization point; schedule independence follows from the join laws, not from the lock.
+
+Selection is a separate deterministic function. It validates candidates against a fixed state, deduplicates exact candidates, sorts by a total order, applies item/unit budgets, selects at most one observation per fact, and assigns labels after ordering. Arrival-time admission is intentionally not used.
+
+## Production adaptations
+
+- Missing evidence digests now use the same exact-text digest as canonical chunks.
+- Connected-runtime identity includes configuration digest, database digest, fusion algorithm version, and RRF constant.
+- Resolved provider settings receive a non-secret configuration fingerprint. Generation and reranking cache namespaces advance to v2 and require that fingerprint.
+- Existing RAG chunks and knowledge facts can be admitted to the provenance kernel without embedding ranks or scores in fact identity.
+- A deterministic tool-evidence ledger demonstrates candidate merge followed by budgeted selection.
+
+## Rejected alternatives
+
+- **Hash arbitrary structs:** concise, but field inclusion becomes accidental and refactors silently alter identity.
+- **Last-write-wins conflicts:** total and fast, but lets arrival order choose truth.
+- **Put score/rank in facts:** prevents stable source identity across queries.
+- **Consume budgets during merge:** makes concurrency and retries semantically observable.
+- **Treat a mutex as determinism:** removes data races but not order-sensitive semantics.
+- **Exclude provider settings from caches:** can return an output produced under a different inference contract.
